      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lakeville was first settled by Peirce in 1705 and grew as a parish of Middleborough in 1717.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakeville%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Assawompsett Pond, a site of a long‑standing Wampanoag settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakeville%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lakeville’s historic library sits behind the old town hall, just across Route 18 from its former spot at Routes 18 and 105.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakeville%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The CapeFlyer seasonal train stops at Lakeville station on the Middleborough/Lakeville border, linking the town to Cape Cod.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakeville%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Ocean Spray, the cranberry giant, has its headquarters in Lakeville, making the town a key player in the cranberry industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lakeville%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Wampanoag Royal Cemetery on a peninsula between Little and Great Quittacas Pond marks a significant indigenous burial ground in modern Lakeville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
